tools={
"concrete-calculator":dict(t="Concrete Calculator – Cubic Yards & Bags for Slabs",d="Free concrete calculator: enter slab length, width and thickness to get cubic yards, cubic feet and the number of 40, 60 or 80 lb (25, 40 or 50 kg) bags needed.",h="Concrete Slab Calculator",
f=[("L","Length (ft)",10),("W","Width (ft)",10),("T","Thickness (in)",4),("X","Waste allowance (%)",10)],
js="""var cf=L*W*(T/12)*(1+X/100),cy=cf/27;return [['Cubic feet',cf.toFixed(2)],['Cubic yards',cy.toFixed(2)],['80 lb bags (0.60 cu ft each)',Math.ceil(cf/0.6)],['60 lb bags (0.45 cu ft each)',Math.ceil(cf/0.45)],['40 lb bags (0.30 cu ft each)',Math.ceil(cf/0.3)]];""",
g="""<h2>How to calculate concrete for a slab</h2><p>Concrete is ordered by volume. Multiply length × width × thickness, keeping every measurement in feet (divide inches by 12). That gives cubic feet; divide by 27 to get cubic yards, the unit ready-mix suppliers use.</p><p><b>Example:</b> a 10 × 10 ft patio, 4 in thick: 10 × 10 × 0.333 = 33.3 cu ft ÷ 27 = 1.23 cu yd. Adding 10% waste gives about 1.36 cu yd.</p><h2>Bags vs. ready-mix truck</h2><p>Typical pre-mixed bag yields: 80 lb ≈ 0.60 cu ft, 60 lb ≈ 0.45 cu ft, 40 lb ≈ 0.30 cu ft; metric bags: 50 kg ≈ 0.0234 m³, 40 kg ≈ 0.0187 m³, 25 kg ≈ 0.0117 m³ (check your bag label—yields vary slightly by brand). For jobs over about 1 cubic yard / 0.75 m³ (45 × 80 lb or about 33 × 50 kg bags), a ready-mix delivery is usually cheaper and gives a more uniform pour.</p><h2>Recommended thickness</h2><ul><li>Walkways and patios: 4 in</li><li>Driveways for passenger cars: 4–5 in; heavier vehicles: 6 in</li><li>Shed bases: 4 in, thickened at the edges</li></ul><h2>Why add waste?</h2><p>Uneven subgrade, form flex and spillage commonly consume 5–10% extra. Running short mid-pour creates a cold joint, so it is cheaper to order a little more.</p>"""),
"paint-calculator":dict(t="Paint Calculator – How Many Gallons Do I Need?",d="Estimate gallons of paint for a room: enter wall dimensions, doors, windows and coats. Uses 350–400 sq ft per gallon coverage.",h="Room Paint Calculator",
f=[("P","Room perimeter (ft) – sum of all wall lengths",48),("H","Wall height (ft)",8),("D","Number of doors",1),("N","Number of windows",2),("C","Coats",2),("V","Coverage per gallon (sq ft)",350)],
js="""var a=Math.max(0,P*H-D*20-N*15),tot=a*C,g=tot/V;return [['Paintable wall area (sq ft)',a.toFixed(0)],['Total area incl. coats',tot.toFixed(0)],['Gallons needed',g.toFixed(2)],['Buy',Math.ceil(g)+' gallon(s)']];""",
g="""<h2>How the paint estimate works</h2><p>Wall area = room perimeter × wall height. Subtract about 20 sq ft per standard door and 15 sq ft per average window. Multiply by the number of coats and divide by the coverage on the can—most interior latex paints state 350–400 sq ft per gallon on smooth surfaces.</p><p><b>Example:</b> a 12 × 12 ft bedroom has a 48 ft perimeter. At 8 ft high: 384 sq ft − 20 (door) − 30 (two windows) = 334 sq ft. Two coats = 668 sq ft ÷ 350 ≈ 1.9 → buy 2 gallons.</p><h2>When to lower the coverage number</h2><ul><li>Bare drywall or fresh plaster: use primer first, or assume ~250–300 sq ft/gal.</li><li>Textured or rough walls absorb more paint.</li><li>Big color changes (dark to light) often need a third coat.</li></ul><h2>Ceilings and trim</h2><p>Ceiling area is simply length × width. Trim usually needs well under a quart for a single room.</p>"""),
"mulch-calculator":dict(t="Mulch Calculator – Cubic Yards & Bags for Garden Beds",d="Calculate how much mulch you need: bed area and depth to cubic yards and 2 or 3 cubic foot bags.",h="Mulch Calculator",
f=[("A","Bed area (sq ft)",200),("T","Depth (in)",3)],
js="""var cf=A*T/12;return [['Cubic feet',cf.toFixed(1)],['Cubic yards',(cf/27).toFixed(2)],['2 cu ft bags',Math.ceil(cf/2)],['3 cu ft bags',Math.ceil(cf/3)]];""",
g="""<h2>Mulch formula</h2><p>Cubic feet = area (sq ft) × depth (in) ÷ 12. Divide by 27 for cubic yards. One cubic yard covers about 108 sq ft at 3 inches, or 162 sq ft at 2 inches.</p><h2>How deep should mulch be?</h2><p>University extension services generally recommend 2–4 inches of organic mulch. Less than 2 in lets weeds through; more than 4 in can starve roots of oxygen. Keep mulch a few inches away from tree trunks—avoid “mulch volcanoes.”</p><h2>Bags or bulk?</h2><p>Bagged mulch is usually sold in 2 cu ft bags (13.5 bags = 1 cu yd). Above roughly 2–3 cubic yards, bulk delivery from a landscape yard is usually cheaper per yard.</p><h2>Measuring irregular beds</h2><p>Split the bed into rectangles (L × W), triangles (½ × base × height) and circles (3.14 × radius²) and add the areas.</p>"""),
"gravel-calculator":dict(t="Gravel Calculator – Tons & Cubic Yards",d="Gravel calculator for driveways, paths and patios: get cubic yards and tons from length, width and depth.",h="Gravel Calculator",
f=[("L","Length (ft)",20),("W","Width (ft)",10),("T","Depth (in)",4),("R","Density (tons per cu yd)",1.4)],
js="""var cy=L*W*T/12/27;return [['Cubic yards',cy.toFixed(2)],['Tons (approx.)',(cy*R).toFixed(2)],['0.5 cu ft bags',Math.ceil(cy*27/0.5)]];""",
g="""<h2>Cubic yards to tons</h2><p>Volume in cubic yards = L × W × depth(in) ÷ 12 ÷ 27. Quarries sell by the ton, so multiply by density. Common crushed stone and pea gravel weigh about 1.3–1.5 tons per cubic yard; 1.4 is a sensible default. Ask your supplier for the exact figure for their material.</p><h2>Typical depths</h2><ul><li>Walkway: 2–3 in of decorative gravel over landscape fabric</li><li>Driveway: 4–6 in base layer (larger crushed stone) plus 2 in top layer</li><li>Patio/paver base: 4 in compacted base</li></ul><h2>Compaction</h2><p>Crushed base compacts roughly 10–15%, so order a little extra for layers you plan to tamp.</p>"""),
"tile-calculator":dict(t="Tile Calculator – How Many Tiles & Boxes Do I Need?",d="Tile calculator for floors and walls: enter area and tile size to get tile count and boxes, with waste for cuts.",h="Tile Calculator",
f=[("L","Area length (ft)",12),("W","Area width (ft)",10),("A","Tile length (in)",12),("B","Tile width (in)",12),("X","Waste (%) – 10 straight, 15 diagonal",10),("S","Sq ft per box",15)],
js="""var area=L*W,ta=A*B/144,n=Math.ceil(area/ta*(1+X/100));return [['Area (sq ft)',area.toFixed(1)],['Tiles needed incl. waste',n],['Boxes',Math.ceil(n*ta/S)]];""",
g="""<h2>Tile formula</h2><p>Tiles = floor area ÷ area of one tile, plus waste. Tile area in sq ft = tile length (in) × width (in) ÷ 144. A 12 × 12 in tile is exactly 1 sq ft.</p><h2>How much waste to add</h2><ul><li>Straight lay, simple room: 10%</li><li>Diagonal or herringbone: 15–20%</li><li>Large-format tiles or many obstacles: 15%+</li></ul><p>Keep a few spare tiles from the same dye lot for future repairs—later batches often differ slightly in shade.</p><h2>Grout joints</h2><p>This estimate ignores grout width, which slightly reduces the tile count—another reason the waste allowance is conservative.</p>"""),
}
